      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7edc17f1-7f6f-544d-9db0-42aef01389da",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Maianthemum canadense\n- Places: Canada, United States, Yukon, British Columbia, Newfoundland, Nebraska, Pennsylvania, Appalachian Mountains, northern Georgia, Lincoln, Omaha, Missouri River\n- Ships: HMCS Mayflower\n- Topics: state geography, naming\n\nFacts:\n- Maianthemum canadense is also known as Canadian may-lily, Canada mayflower, false lily-of-the-valley, Canadian lily-of-the-valley, wild lily-of-the-valley, and Two-leaved Solomonseal.\n- Maianthemum canadense is a dominant understory perennial flowering plant native to sub-boreal conifer forests in Canada and the northern United States.\n- Maianthemum canadense ranges from Yukon and British Columbia east to Newfoundland and south to Nebraska and Pennsylvania, and it also occurs in the Appalachian Mountains to northern Georgia.\n- Nebraska is the only triply landlocked U.S. state.\n- Nebraska's state capital is Lincoln.\n- Nebraska's largest city is Omaha, which is on the Missouri River.\n- HMCS Mayflower was named after the flowering plant Maianthemum canadense.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "fe16f7df-7ba3-50f9-be2f-5782ef80c9b6",
        "text": "Page: Maianthemum canadense. Sentence: Maianthemum canadense (Canadian may-lily, Canada mayflower, false lily-of-the-valley, Canadian lily-of-the-valley, wild lily-of-the-valley, Two-leaved Solomonseal) is a dominant understory perennial flowering plant, native to the sub-boreal conifer forests in Canada and the northern United States, from Yukon and British Columbia east to Newfoundland and south to Nebraska and Pennsylvania, and also in the Appalachian Mountains to northern Georgia.\nPage: Nebraska. Sentence: It is the only triply landlocked U.S. state.\nPage: Nebraska. Sentence: Its state capital is Lincoln, and its largest city is Omaha, which is on the Missouri River.\nPage: HMCS Mayflower. Sentence: She was named after the flowering plant \"Maianthemum canadense\".",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
